  <!-- How It Works -->
  <section class="msv__section">
    <div class="msv__container--narrow">
      <div class="msv__section-hd">
        <h2>How the NLV Income Calculation Works</h2>
        <p>The Spanish Non-Lucrative Visa income requirement is tied to the IPREM index and changes annually based on Spanish government updates.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="msv__alert msv__alert--info">
        <span class="msv__alert-icon">ℹ️</span>
        <p><strong>IPREM Explained:</strong> The IPREM (Indicador Público de Renta de Efectos Múltiples) is Spain's official income index used to calculate benefits and thresholds. For 2026, the baseline IPREM is €600/month. This index updates every January 1st and is published in Spain's Official Gazette.</p>
      </div>

      <h3 style="margin-top: 2rem;">Income Threshold Formula</h3>
      <p>The NLV income requirement follows this straightforward formula:</p>

      <div style="background: #f9fafb; border-left: 4px solid var(--red); padding: 1.5rem; margin: 1.5rem 0; border-radius: 6px;">
        <p style="margin: 0; font-weight: 700; color: var(--navy);">Single Applicant: 400% IPREM = €2,400/month (€28,800/year)</p>
        <p style="margin: 0.8rem 0 0 0; font-weight: 700; color: var(--navy);">Each Additional Family Member: +100% IPREM = +€600/month (+€7,200/year)</p>
      </div>

      <h3 style="margin-top: 2rem;">Income Thresholds by Family Size</h3>
      <table class="msv__income-threshold-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th class="family-size">Family Size</th>
            <th>Monthly Requirement (2026)</th>
            <th>Annual Requirement</th>
            <th>Savings Equivalent (12 months)</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="family-size">Single Applicant</td>
            <td>€2,400</td>
            <td>€28,800</td>
            <td>€28,800</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="family-size">Couple (2 members)</td>
            <td>€3,000</td>
            <td>€36,000</td>
            <td>€36,000</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="family-size">Family of 3</td>
            <td>€3,600</td>
            <td>€43,200</td>
            <td>€43,200</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="family-size">Family of 4</td>
            <td>€4,200</td>
            <td>€50,400</td>
            <td>€50,400</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h3 style="margin-top: 2rem;">What Counts Toward the Income Threshold</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Pension Income:</strong> Government pensions, private pension plans, retirement annuities</li>
        <li><strong>Rental Income:</strong> Income from rental properties in Spain or abroad</li>
        <li><strong>Investment Income:</strong> Dividend income, bond interest, investment fund returns</li>
        <li><strong>Other Passive Income:</strong> Royalties, licensing fees, business profits from passive ventures</li>
        <li><strong>Savings as Income:</strong> Can be assessed as equivalent to 12 months of required income</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="msv__alert msv__alert--warn">
        <span class="msv__alert-icon">⚠️</span>
        <p><strong>What Does NOT Count:</strong> Active employment income, freelance/self-employment income, spousal income (unless spouse is applying), state benefits, or unverifiable informal income. All income must be documented with bank statements, tax returns, and official income verification letters.</p>
      </div>

      <h3 style="margin-top: 2rem;">Savings as an Income Equivalent</h3>
      <p>Spanish consulates recognize that applicants may have substantial savings instead of regular income. The standard assessment is:</p>

      <div style="background: #f9fafb; border-left: 4px solid var(--red); padding: 1.5rem; margin: 1.5rem 0; border-radius: 6px;">
        <p style="margin: 0; font-weight: 700; color: var(--navy);">Savings Threshold = 12 months of required income</p>
        <p style="margin: 0.8rem 0 0 0; color: var(--black); font-size: 0.95rem;">Example: A single applicant requiring €28,800 annually should have at least €28,800 in liquid savings to demonstrate financial independence.</p>
      </div>

      <h3 style="margin-top: 2rem;">Exchange Rate Risk & Buffer Recommendations</h3>
      <p>If your income is in a foreign currency, exchange rate fluctuations can affect your eligibility. Consider these recommendations:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>5-10% Buffer:</strong> If your income is borderline, maintain a 5-10% surplus to account for currency fluctuations and unforeseen expenses</li>
        <li><strong>GBP/USD Volatility:</strong> Sterling and US dollar exchange rates fluctuate significantly. Lock in documentation at current favorable rates</li>
        <li><strong>Currency Conversion:</strong> Spanish authorities will use official exchange rates from the application date. Budget for potential adverse movements</li>
        <li><strong>Multi-Currency Income:</strong> If income arrives in multiple currencies, combine them at current rates. Document each source separately</li>
        <li><strong>Long-Term Stability:</strong> The NLV requires proof of financial stability for at least 1 year. Ensure your income sources are durable, not temporary</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 style="margin-top: 2rem;">How to Improve Your Financial Position Before Applying</h3>
      <p>If your current income falls short, consider these strategies:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Maximize Pension Income:</strong> If eligible for early pension withdrawals, claim them before application</li>
        <li><strong>Generate Rental Income:</strong> If you own property, consider renting it and documenting the income consistently for 12+ months</li>
        <li><strong>Liquidate Investments:</strong> If you have volatile investments, convert them to income-generating assets (bonds, dividends)</li>
        <li><strong>Spousal Applications:</strong> Apply jointly if your partner has qualifying income. Each applicant is assessed separately</li>
        <li><strong>Build Savings:</strong> Even without sufficient income, substantial savings (12x your shortfall) can compensate</li>
        <li><strong>Document Everything:</strong> Start gathering bank statements, income verification letters, and tax returns 12 months before applying</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 style="margin-top: 2rem;">Common Calculation Mistakes to Avoid</h3>
      <div class="msv__alert msv__alert--red">
        <span class="msv__alert-icon">❌</span>
        <p><strong>Mistake #1:</strong> Including active employment or freelance income. NLV strictly requires passive income. Self-employment does not qualify.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="msv__alert msv__alert--red">
        <span class="msv__alert-icon">❌</span>
        <p><strong>Mistake #2:</strong> Not converting foreign income at the correct date. Use the official exchange rate from your application date, not historical rates.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="msv__alert msv__alert--red">
        <span class="msv__alert-icon">❌</span>
        <p><strong>Mistake #3:</strong> Mixing gross and net income. Always use net income after taxes for income calculations.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="msv__alert msv__alert--red">
        <span class="msv__alert-icon">❌</span>
        <p><strong>Mistake #4:</strong> Forgetting that each family member increases the threshold. The requirement scales linearly—don't underestimate for large families.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="msv__alert msv__alert--red">
        <span class="msv__alert-icon">❌</span>
        <p><strong>Mistake #5:</strong> Submitting unverified income. Every income source must be backed by official documentation—bank statements, tax certificates, and notarized letters from income providers.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- FAQ Section -->
  <section class="msv__section--cream">
    <div class="msv__container--narrow">
      <div class="msv__section-hd">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <p>Get answers to the most common questions about NLV income calculations and requirements.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="msv__faq-list">
        <details>
          <summary>What is the 2026 IPREM income requirement for a single NLV applicant?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>For a single applicant in 2026, the Non-Lucrative Visa requires €2,400 per month (400% of IPREM, which is €600). This equals approximately €28,800 per year. This calculation uses the official 2026 IPREM index published by the Spanish government and updated annually on January 1st.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>How much additional income is needed for each family member?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Each additional family member requires an additional 100% IPREM, or approximately €600 per month. For example, a couple would need €3,000 per month (€2,400 + €600), and a family of four would need €4,200 per month. Partners and dependent children count as family members; elderly parents may also qualify depending on consulate guidelines.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>Can I use savings to meet the income requirement?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Yes, savings can be used to satisfy the threshold, but only at a rate of 12 months of required income. For a single applicant needing €28,800 annually, you would need €28,800 in liquid savings. The savings must be in a personal bank account with clear documentation of source and availability.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>Which types of passive income qualify for the NLV?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Qualifying income sources include: pension benefits (government or private), rental income from properties, investment income (dividends, interest, capital gains), annuities, and other passive income streams. Income must be legally documented and stable. Active employment, freelance work, and business income do NOT qualify for the NLV.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>How do I convert foreign currency income to EUR for the NLV application?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Convert foreign currency income using the official exchange rate on the date of your application submission. Spanish consulates typically use the ECB (European Central Bank) daily rate or the Bank of Spain's published rates. Your calculator above supports GBP, USD, AUD, CAD, and ZAR conversions. Always maintain a 5-10% buffer above the minimum to account for currency fluctuations between documentation and application submission.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>Do I need to prove 12 months of income history for the NLV?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Yes, most Spanish consulates require 12 months of bank statements and income verification to prove that your income is stable and will continue. Pension income typically requires notarized letters from the pension provider. Rental income requires 12 months of rental contracts and bank deposits. Investment income requires brokerage statements. Start gathering documentation well in advance of your application.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>What happens if my income exceeds the NLV requirement?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Exceeding the minimum income requirement strengthens your application significantly. There is no maximum income limit for the NLV. If you have income above €2,400 (single) or above your family threshold, you satisfy the financial requirement completely. Your consulate may be more likely to approve quickly and for the maximum visa duration (1-2 years).</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>Can I combine my partner's income if we're applying together?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>No, each applicant's income is assessed individually. You cannot pool spousal income. However, if both partners have qualifying passive income, each can use their own income to satisfy their portion of the requirement. If only one partner has sufficient income, the other can use savings or a combination of personal income and savings.</p>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
